# support/

Runtime support data for RetDec Imortek (v2.0.22): PE ordinal databases,
YARA tool/packer/compiler patterns, and (when fetched) type JSON used by
fileinfo / static-code detection.

This directory is **not** the decompiler pipeline. Installers and
`cmake --install` copy a processed tree to `share/retdec/support/`. Large
files that are gitignored are pulled once with
`scripts/fetch-large-files.sh` (Linux/macOS/WSL) or
`scripts/fetch-large-files.ps1` (Windows). Those scripts download from the
upstream RetDec mirror (`avast/retdec`); that is data provenance, not an
Avast-only build.

## Layout

| Path | Role |
|------|------|
| `ordinals/` | PE export ordinal → name maps (`x86/`, `arm/`) |
| `yara_patterns/` | YARA sources for tools, packers, compilers, signsrch |
| `types/` | OS type JSON (`windows.json`, `linux.json`, `windrivers.json`) — fetched, not all committed |
| `install-yara.py` | Compile/install YARA patterns with `retdec-yarac` at install time |
| `install-share.py` | Fetch the versioned support tarball into `share/retdec/support` |
| `CMakeLists.txt` | Single install script for ordinals + YARA + share package |
| `models.json` / `models.md` | Metadata for optional GGUF neural models (llama.cpp is opt-in; not required) |

YARA **compilation** at install time uses in-tree `retdec-yarac` (YARA sources
live under `deps/yara`). It is driven by `support/CMakeLists.txt` and
`install-yara.py`, not by a separate Avast website step.

`scripts/regenerate-retdec-support.sh` is **reference only**. Four-toolchain
signature regen is out of maintainer scope; the tarball pin in
`cmake/deps.cmake` is sufficient.

## How it is installed

A normal product configure already wires this. Example:

```bash
cmake --preset full-linux-release
cmake --build --preset full-linux-release
cmake --install build/linux
```

`RETDEC_ENABLE_SUPPORT_ORDINALS`, `RETDEC_ENABLE_SUPPORT_YARA_TOOLS`, and
related options are set by the full presets. You do not compile YARA rules
by hand for a GUI or CLI install.

First-time large-file fetch (before the first build):

```bash
bash scripts/fetch-large-files.sh
```
